Chapter: EcdsaP256Test
  cites Foreword chapter EcdsaP256
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 A P-256 signature nobody here produced, checked here.

 This is the direction the compression post-mortem says to write first:
 their bytes, our reader. Codex cannot sign with this curve and does not
 want to, so there is no paired signer to agree with and no way for this
 test to pass by two halves sharing an assumption.

 The vector is RFC 6979 A.2.5, the published deterministic-ECDSA answer
 for P-256 with SHA-256 over the message "sample". It was confirmed
 against TWO independent implementations before anything here was
 written -- OpenSSL 3.0.13 `dgst -sha256 -verify` and .NET's ECDsa on
 Windows CNG -- and both also reject the perturbations below. A
 transcription error in the curve constants or in this vector therefore
 has somewhere to show up, which a single-oracle check could not offer.

 We say:

Section: Vectors

 The public key as SEC1 uncompressed: 0x04 and then the two coordinates.

  p-pub : List Integer =
    [4, 96, 254, 212, 186, 37, 90, 157, 49, 201, 97, 235, 116, 198, 53, 109,
     104, 192, 73, 184, 146, 59, 97, 250, 108, 230, 105, 98, 46, 96, 242, 159,
     182, 121, 3, 254, 16, 8, 184, 188, 153, 164, 26, 233, 233, 86, 40, 188,
     100, 242, 241, 178, 12, 45, 126, 159, 81, 119, 163, 194, 148, 212, 70, 34,
     153]

 "sample", which is the message RFC 6979 signs.

  p-msg : List Integer =
    [115, 97, 109, 112, 108, 101]

 r then s, thirty-two bytes each, which is the fixed-width form TLS and
 the JOSE family use. A certificate carries the DER form instead; taking
 that apart is the X.509 layer's job and not this chapter's.

  p-sig : List Integer =
    [239, 212, 139, 42, 172, 182, 168, 253, 17, 64, 221, 156, 212, 94, 129, 214,
     157, 44, 135, 123, 86, 170, 249, 145, 195, 77, 14, 168, 78, 175, 55, 22,
     247, 203, 28, 148, 45, 101, 124, 65, 212, 54, 199, 161, 182, 226, 159, 101,
     243, 233, 0, 219, 185, 175, 244, 6, 77, 196, 171, 47, 132, 58, 205, 168]

Section: Cases

 The positive.

  ok-case : Boolean = ecdsa-p256-verify p-pub p-msg p-sig

 Every altered byte below is produced by flipping a bit of the original
 rather than by writing a literal. A control that quietly tests nothing
 is worse than no control here, because these are the lines that would
 report a forgery as genuine, and the RSA test next door has already been
 bitten once by a "different" value that happened to equal the original.

 One bit of s. The signature is still the right width and still a scalar
 in range, so only the arithmetic can reject it.

  bad-sig : List Integer = ep-flip p-sig 63
  bad-sig-case : Boolean = ecdsa-p256-verify p-pub p-msg bad-sig

 One bit of r. This exercises a different half of the check: r feeds the
 second scalar multiplication AND is the value the recovered x is
 compared against, so a wrong r is wrong twice over.

  bad-r : List Integer = ep-flip p-sig 0
  bad-r-case : Boolean = ecdsa-p256-verify p-pub p-msg bad-r

 The genuine signature against a different message.

  other-msg : List Integer = ep-flip p-msg 0
  wrong-msg-case : Boolean = ecdsa-p256-verify p-pub other-msg p-sig

 s of zero, refused on range before any arithmetic. SEC1 requires the
 range check and it is not decoration: a zero scalar sails through the
 inversion and the comparison below can be made to hold.

  zero-s : List Integer = ep-zero-tail p-sig 32
  zero-s-case : Boolean = ecdsa-p256-verify p-pub p-msg zero-s

 A public key one bit off the curve.

 THIS LINE DOES NOT EXERCISE THE CURVE CHECK, and saying so is the point
 of writing it down. Run with `ec-on-curve` forced to True the output of
 this whole test is byte-identical: a tampered key fails the arithmetic
 anyway, so the end-to-end case cannot tell a guard that holds from a
 guard that does nothing. The same is true of the zero-scalar case above,
 which yields the point at infinity and is refused there whether or not
 the range check runs.

 So the two guards are asserted directly, below, where they can fail.
 The end-to-end lines stay because they pin the answer; they are simply
 not evidence about these two predicates.

  bad-pub : List Integer = ep-flip p-pub 64
  bad-pub-case : Boolean = ecdsa-p256-verify bad-pub p-msg p-sig

Section: The Two Guards, Asserted Where They Can Fail

  p-field : EcField = ec-p256-curve

  good-point : EcPoint = ec-point-of (ec-slice p-pub 1 32) (ec-slice p-pub 33 32) p-field
  bad-point : EcPoint = ec-point-of (ec-slice bad-pub 1 32) (ec-slice bad-pub 33 32) p-field

  good-on-curve : Boolean = ec-on-curve good-point p-field
  bad-on-curve : Boolean = ec-on-curve bad-point p-field

 The range check, both directions. A genuine s is in [1, n-1]; zero is
 not, and neither is n itself, which is the boundary the comparison has
 to get right and the one an off-by-one would let through.

  good-scalar : Boolean = ec-scalar-ok (ec-slice p-sig 32 32) 32 ec-n-bytes
  zero-scalar : Boolean = ec-scalar-ok (ep-zero-tail (ec-slice p-sig 32 32) 0) 32 ec-n-bytes
  order-scalar : Boolean = ec-scalar-ok ec-n-bytes 32 ec-n-bytes

 A signature of the wrong width, refused before anything else runs.

  short-sig : List Integer = ep-take p-sig 63 0 []
  short-case : Boolean = ecdsa-p256-verify p-pub p-msg short-sig

Section: Helpers

  ep-take : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ep-take (bs) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else ep-take bs n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs i))

  ep-copy : List Integer -> List Integer
  ep-copy (bs) = ep-take bs (list-length bs) 0 []

  ep-flip : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  ep-flip (bs) (i) = list-set-at (ep-copy bs) i (bit-xor (list-at bs i) 1)

  ep-zero-tail : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  ep-zero-tail (bs) (from) = ep-zero-loop (ep-copy bs) from

  ep-zero-loop : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  ep-zero-loop (bs) (i) =
    if i >= list-length bs then bs
    else ep-zero-loop (list-set-at bs i 0) (i + 1)

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("rfc6979 signature verifies = " & show ok-case)
    print-line-uni ("one bit flipped in s = " & show bad-sig-case)
    print-line-uni ("one bit flipped in r = " & show bad-r-case)
    print-line-uni ("signature against another message = " & show wrong-msg-case)
    print-line-uni ("s of zero = " & show zero-s-case)
    print-line-uni ("public key off the curve = " & show bad-pub-case)
    print-line-uni ("signature of the wrong width = " & show short-case)
    print-line-uni ("genuine key is on the curve = " & show good-on-curve)
    print-line-uni ("tampered key is on the curve = " & show bad-on-curve)
    print-line-uni ("genuine s is in range = " & show good-scalar)
    print-line-uni ("zero s is in range = " & show zero-scalar)
    print-line-uni ("s equal to the order is in range = " & show order-scalar)
  end

